TryHackMe: Introduction to SOAR

Platform: TryHackMe
Room: Introduction to SOAR
Difficulty: Easy
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn the fundamentals of Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response (SOAR), including how SOAR platforms automate security workflows, improve incident response efficiency, and support SOC operations through orchestration and automated playbooks.

Step 1: Understanding SOAR

The room introduced SOAR as a security solution designed to help security teams automate repetitive tasks and streamline incident response processes.

SOAR stands for:

Security
Orchestration
Automation
Response

SOAR platforms integrate multiple security tools and allow analysts to automate workflows that would otherwise require manual effort.

Main SOAR functions:

Security automation
Incident response orchestration
Workflow management
Alert handling
Threat intelligence integration
Case management

SOAR solutions help SOC teams reduce response times and improve operational efficiency.

Step 2: Why SOAR Is Important

The room explained that modern SOC environments generate massive numbers of alerts every day.

Challenges faced by analysts:

Alert fatigue
Manual investigations
Repetitive tasks
Slow incident response
Large volumes of security events

SOAR helps address these issues through automation.

Benefits of SOAR:

Faster response times
Reduced manual workload
Improved consistency
Better collaboration
Automated investigations
Increased SOC efficiency

SOAR platforms allow analysts to focus on higher-priority threats instead of repetitive operational tasks.

Step 3: Orchestration

The room introduced orchestration as the process of connecting and coordinating multiple security tools and systems.

Examples of integrated tools:

SIEM platforms
Firewalls
EDR solutions
Threat intelligence platforms
Ticketing systems
Email security tools

Orchestration allows SOAR platforms to:

Share information between tools
Trigger automated actions
Centralize investigations
Improve incident workflows

This integration improves visibility and enables faster threat containment.

Step 4: Automation

One of the core features of SOAR is automation.

Automation helps eliminate repetitive security tasks.

Examples of automated actions:

Blocking malicious IP addresses
Isolating compromised endpoints
Sending alerts to analysts
Enriching threat intelligence data
Creating incident tickets
Gathering forensic information

Automation reduces human error and speeds up security operations.

The room demonstrated how workflows can automatically trigger actions based on predefined conditions.

Step 5: Incident Response Playbooks

The room explained how SOAR platforms use playbooks to automate incident response procedures.

Playbooks are predefined workflows that guide automated responses to security events.

Common playbook actions:

Collect evidence
Query threat intelligence feeds
Analyze indicators of compromise (IOCs)
Notify analysts
Escalate incidents
Contain threats

Examples of playbooks:

Phishing response
Malware detection
Brute-force attack handling
Suspicious login investigation

Playbooks ensure consistent and repeatable incident response processes.

Step 6: Threat Intelligence Integration

The room covered how SOAR platforms integrate with threat intelligence sources.

Threat intelligence integration allows analysts to:

Enrich alerts with contextual data
Check IP reputation
Investigate domains and hashes
Identify known malicious indicators

SOAR platforms can automatically pull intelligence from external sources and attach it to incidents for faster investigations.

This helps analysts make more informed decisions during incident response.

Step 7: SOAR Use Cases

The room introduced common real-world SOAR applications.

Examples of SOAR use cases:

Phishing email triage
Malware response automation
Alert enrichment
User account investigations
Threat hunting support
Ticket automation
Security alert prioritization

SOAR platforms improve scalability within SOC environments by automating repetitive security operations.

